{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-18_14-39-49-dragonboat_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nv=[x for x in s['interpretations'] if x['id'].startswith('5ccfde')][0]\nprint(v['rationale']); print('---'); print(v['limitations'])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-18_14-39-49-dragonboat_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nv=[x for x in s['interpretations'] if x['id'].startswith('5ccfde')][0]\nprint(v['rationale']); print('---'); print(v['limitations'])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"The suspicion is refuted for the captured raft paths. The hazard requires a leader whose log holds a committed configuration change that its membership maps have not yet applied, because tryCommit computes the quorum from those maps and a second change committed before the first is applied would be committed with the pre-change quorum. Two gates prevent that history. A leader that proposed a configuration change sets pendingConfigChange in handleLeaderPropose and keeps it set until a configuration-change apply path clears it, so any further configuration change it proposes is reported dropped and replaced with an empty application entry; the leader cannot accumulate a second uncommitted change while the first is committed but unapplied. A node that did not propose the change can only become leader by campaigning, and handleNodeElection refuses to campaign while log.committed exceeds the applied index, so a node that still has any committed unapplied entry, including a configuration change, cannot become leader; TimeoutNow cannot bypass this because it only advances the election tick and lets the normal tick emit the Election message that handleNodeElection gates. preLeaderPromotionHandleConfigChange therefore runs only after the applied index has caught up with the committed index, and its count of entries after the committed index is consistent with that state; it re-sets the pending flag for the single uncommitted change it can legitimately find and panics if the log ever holds more than one. The consequence is an availability tradeoff rather than the suspected safety violation: while the applied index lags, the node declines to campaign, and a leader keeps dropping configuration changes until the in-flight change is applied. The low-level pieces that suggested the hazard remain exactly as described, but the permitted leadership and proposal histories do not produce the required state.\n---\n['This is a source-only account; no execution of the leadership, proposal or apply ordering is retained for it.', 'Direct mutation of the internal role maps, an operator starting a node with a role inconsistent with cluster membership, forged messages, and a custom transport that changes message admission are excluded.', 'The gate is a liveness tradeoff: a node whose applied index lags cannot campaign and a leader keeps dropping configuration changes, and no bound on that delay is established.', 'Snapshot restore and configuration changes carried by snapshots are covered only by the role guards recorded elsewhere, not by this account.']\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
